  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Solve for $a$ where $\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a}=\\frac{7}{10a}.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We note that $a=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $a,\\,5a,$ and $10a.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a,5a,10a)=10a.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10a,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a} & =\\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10a(\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a}) & =10a(\\frac{7}{10a}) \\\\ 10a\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{a}+10a\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{5a} & =10a\\cdot \\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10\\cdot a\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{a}+2\\cdot 5a\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{5a} & =10a\\cdot \\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{\\cancel{a}}+2\\cdot \\cancel{5a}\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{\\cancel{5a}} & =\\cancel{10a}\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{10a}} \\\\ 10(a+5)+2(2-a) & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(a+5)+2(2-a) & =7 \\\\ 10a+50+4-2a & =7 \\\\ 8a+54 & =7 \\\\ 8a & =-47 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{47}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. However, since $a=-\\frac{47}{8}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $a=-\\frac{47}{8}.$",
